**What was already in my context when I arrived:** the global `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md` (it describes this review machinery, `review-code` and rule 18), the user's email address, and a SessionStart hook message about Ants MCP. No project `CLAUDE.md`, memory index or git snapshot. The project rules I used came from the shared context's summary only.

I did not open the streaming-upload spec. No lead touches upload: L1 is only about how the request is signed.

---

### Lead L1
**VERDICT: real, but no current caller is affected (latent).**

`s3.c:466` — `return strcmp(*(const char* const*)a, *(const char* const*)b);` compares the whole `name=value` string. SigV4 sorts by encoded name first. When one name extends another, the comparison reaches `=` (0x3D) on the shorter name, and `=` sorts after digits, `-`, `.` and `%`. Example: `a=1` against `a1=2`. SigV4 puts `a` first; strcmp compares `=` with `1` and puts `a1=2` first. The signature would then be wrong and S3 would answer 403.

Callers checked: there is one, `s3.c:801`. The strings that reach it:
- `"uploads="` (`:1799-1800`)
- `"uploadId=%s"` (`:1498`, `:1599`)
- `"partNumber=%u&uploadId=%s"` (`:1701`)

None of these names is a prefix of another, so no request signed today is wrong. The comment at `:457-462` says plain strcmp "gives the spec-mandated order". That comment is false: the code side is wrong, not the spec.

### Lead L2
**VERDICT: real (it already existed in upstream; the fork made it worse).**

`webdav_create_digest_auth` calls `webdav_cleanup_digest()` at `webdav.c:269` before parsing. Several exits then `return false` without going through `parse_fail` and leave the digest state half-filled:
- realm quote fails (`:285`)
- qop has no closing quote, or qop lacks "auth" (`if (!webdav_st->qop_auth) return false;`)
- nonce, opaque or algorithm quote fails
- algorithm `malloc` fails
- an unknown parameter has an unterminated quote

On every one of those exits `cnonce` is NULL, because `webdav_create_cnonce` (`:370`) only runs on the success path. `realm`, `nonce` and `ha1hash` may also be NULL.

`basic` is set true only in `webdav_sync_begin` (`:830`) and set false on success (`:376`); a failure never puts it back. The state is used afterwards:
- Once one challenge in a sync has succeeded, `webdav_get_auth_header` (`:563`) takes the digest branch for every later request: GET `:914`, PUT `:1231`, MKCOL `:1050`, COPY, MOVE, DELETE, OPTIONS `:743`.
- That reaches `webdav_create_digest_auth_header`, which runs `strlen(webdav_st->cnonce)` unconditionally, plus `strlen(webdav_st->realm)` and `strlen(webdav_st->nonce)`.
- It also reaches `webdav_create_ha1`, which runs `strdup(webdav_st->ha1hash)`.

Each of those is a NULL dereference. So a later 401 whose Digest challenge this parser rejects crashes RetroArch on the next request. Two examples that trigger it:
- `qop="auth-int"` on a stale-nonce challenge
- a value with an unterminated quote

Upstream's `cnonce` was the literal `"1a2b3c4f"` and could never be NULL. The fork's change to a heap `cnonce` made the crash certain on these paths. The `realm`/`nonce` half of the problem is upstream's.

### Lead L3
**VERDICT: not a defect in `cloud_sync_http_body_is_framed`. The RFC gap is in upstream `net_http`, and it is real but low.**

`net_http.c:2319-2338` reads digits and stops at the first non-digit. It errors only on `!any || oflow`. So `Content-Length: 123abc` already frames the body as 123 bytes. The helper (`cloud_sync_driver.c:159-168`) uses the same rule and checks against the same 123, so the two agree and a truncated body is still caught. Leading whitespace is handled the same way in both: `net_http_header_value` skips spaces and tabs (`net_http.c:3566-3567`), as the parser does.

RFC 9112 §6.3 item 5 says an invalid Content-Length is an unrecoverable error. That check belongs in `net_http`'s parser, and `libretro-common` is vendored code.

One related case fails safe. With duplicate Content-Length headers, `net_http` frames by the last one but `net_http_header_value` returns the first. When they differ, the helper rejects the response. That direction loses no data.

### Lead L4
**VERDICT: not a defect (it fails safe). The header comment overstates what the helper accepts.**

`net_http` does not drop Content-Length from the stored headers. The Content-Length case ends in `break`, so the line is stored with the rest. Transfer-Encoding still wins for framing (`net_http.c:2345`, `2359`), and `len` is reset (`:2267-2281`).

So the helper finds the Content-Length and compares it with the de-chunked length. A response carrying both headers is usually rejected. RFC 9112 §6.3 item 3 says such a message "ought to be handled as an error", so rejecting it conforms. A match by coincidence is also safe, because chunked framing needs the terminating chunk.

The doc side is the wrong side: `cloud_sync_driver.h:56-59` says "or it was sent chunked" without this exception.

### Lead S5
**VERDICT: not a defect (no link failure).**

Every `HAVE_S3` use I found is in one of three places:
- nested inside `HAVE_CLOUDSYNC`: `configuration.c:1959`, `configuration.h:1254`, `griffin.c:1846`, and `menu_setting.c:11905`, `14237`, `18347`
- in a file that is only built when `HAVE_CLOUDSYNC` is on: `cloud_sync_driver.c:37`, which is the only reference to `cloud_sync_s3`
- an extern declaration only: `cloud_sync_driver.h:66`

One use is outside the guard: `menu_displaylist.c:12846`, in the unguarded `case DISPLAYLIST_CLOUD_SYNC_SETTINGS_LIST` (`:12832`). It references only `MENU_ENUM_LABEL_*` values, which are not link symbols.

What remains is that `--enable-s3` without `HAVE_CLOUDSYNC` is silently ignored. Makefile.common sets that flag only for `Linux`/`Win` (`:2464-2470`). I did not settle whether that block compiles; see Open questions.

